  [38;5;153mmsg[39m is always a developer-authored literal — never interpolated user data. Everything variable goes in [38;5;153mctx[39m, which is
  the only field the allowlist governs. Keeping that separation is what makes the allowlist meaningful; the moment
  someone writes [38;5;153mlog.error(\[39mlogin failed for ${username}`)`, redaction is bypassed, so the spec will call that out
  explicitly.

  [1mThe allowlist.[0m [38;5;153mredact(ctx)[39m walks the top level of [38;5;153mctx[39m and keeps only keys in [38;5;153mALLOWED_CTX_FIELDS[39m — starting set:
  [38;5;153maction[39m, [38;5;153mcomponent[39m, [38;5;153mcode[39m, [38;5;153mstatus[39m, [38;5;153mfield[39m, [38;5;153mdurationMs[39m, [38;5;153mattempt[39m, [38;5;153merrorName[39m, [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m, [38;5;153mstack[39m. Everything else is
  dropped regardless of name, and the count of dropped keys is recorded as [38;5;153mctx._dropped[39m so you can tell from the logs
  that redaction fired. Values are coerced: strings truncated to 512 characters, numbers and booleans pass through, [38;5;153mnull
[39m  passes through, anything else (objects, arrays, functions) becomes [38;5;153m"[dropped:object]"[39m rather than being walked. Not
  recursing is deliberate — a nested walk is where a password in [38;5;153mformData.fields[2].value[39m would sneak through.

  [1mSession correlation.[0m A 16-hex-character random ID in [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, so you can follow one person's sequence of
  failures within a tab without storing who they are. It resets when the tab closes and is never sent to your login
  endpoint — only to the log endpoint. This replaces the current plaintext username logging.

  [1mThe open risk, and it's a real one.[0m [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m and [38;5;153mstack[39m are on the allowlist above, because without them a crash
  report is nearly useless — you get "something threw" and no location. But error messages are the one allowlisted field
  that can contain arbitrary user input: a validation library that throws [38;5;153mInvalid value: hunter2[39m, or a JSON parse error
  echoing the malformed payload, will carry that text straight to your log store. The allowlist cannot protect you
  here, because the field is allowed by name and the danger is in its value. Truncation limits the blast radius but
  doesn't prevent it.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Error text [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mHow should error messages and stack traces be handled on the wire?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mAllowlist both, truncated (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246merrorName, errorMessage, stack all sent, capped at 512 chars. Crash reports stay useful; accepts that a message may
[39m     [38;5;246membed user input.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Name + stack only, drop message
     [38;5;246mSend the error class and stack frames but never the message text. Removes the main leak path; makes some errors [39m
     [38;5;246mmuch harder to identify.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Name only, no message or stack
     [38;5;246mStrictest. Near-zero leak risk, and near-useless for the production debugging this whole change exists to enable.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
